Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether every clopen subset of a t.d.l.c. group is a union of cosets of some compact open subgroup. I'll construct a counterexample in $\mathbb{Q}_p$.

## Proof

**Answer: No.** There exists a t.d.l.c. group $G$ and a clopen subset $U$ such that no neighborhood $V$ of the identity satisfies $gV \subset U$ for all $g \in U$.

### The equivalence

First, we verify the stated equivalence. If $U = \bigcup_i g_i K$ for a compact open subgroup $K$, then for any $g \in U$, we have $g \in g_iK$ for some $i$, so $gK \subseteq g_iK \subseteq U$; take $V = K$. Conversely, if $gV \subseteq U$ for all $g \in U$, since $G$ is t.d.l.c. there is a compact open subgroup $K \subseteq V$, giving $gK \subseteq U$ for all $g \in U$, so $U = \bigcup_{g \in U} gK$.

### Counterexample

Let $G = \mathbb{Q}_p$ (the field of $p$-adic numbers), which is a t.d.l.c. group under addition. Its compact open subgroups are exactly $p^N\mathbb{Z}_p$ for $N \geq 0$.

Define:
$$U = \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} \left(p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p\right).$$

**$U$ is open:** It is a union of open balls $p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p = \{x \in \mathbb{Q}_p : v_p(x - p^{-n}) \geq n\}$.

**$U$ is closed:** We show the complement is open. Take $x \notin U$.

- *Case $v_p(x) \geq 0$* (i.e., $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$): Every element of $U$ has valuation $-n < 0$ for some $n \geq 1$ (since for $y \in p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$, we have $v_p(y) = \min(-n, v_p(y - p^{-n})) = -n$ because $v_p(p^{-n}) = -n < n \leq v_p(y - p^{-n})$). So $U \cap \mathbb{Z}_p = \emptyset$, and the open ball $x + p\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$ is disjoint from $U$.

- *Case $v_p(x) = -n$ for some $n \geq 1$*: The elements of $U$ with valuation $-n$ are exactly those in $p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$ (by the same valuation argument: for $m \neq n$ with $m \geq 1$, if $y \in p^{-m} + p^m\mathbb{Z}_p$ then $v_p(y) = -m \neq -n$). Since $x \notin U$, we have $x \notin p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$. The coset $x + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$ is disjoint from $p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$ (distinct cosets of the same subgroup), and all its elements have valuation $-n$, so it is disjoint from all of $U$.

In both cases, $x$ has an open neighborhood disjoint from $U$. So $U^c$ is open, hence $U$ is closed.

**$U$ is not a union of cosets of any compact open subgroup:** Suppose for contradiction that $U$ is a union of cosets of $p^N\mathbb{Z}_p$ for some $N \geq 0$. This means $U + p^N\mathbb{Z}_p = U$, i.e., for every $x \in U$, the entire coset $x + p^N\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq U$.

Choose any $n > N$ (which exists since $N$ is fixed). The point $p^{-n} \in U$ (it is the center of the ball $p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$). So we need:
$$p^{-n} + p^N\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq U.$$

Now, every element $y \in p^{-n} + p^N\mathbb{Z}_p$ satisfies $v_p(y) = -n$ (since $v_p(p^{-n}) = -n < N \leq v_p(y - p^{-n})$). The only elements of $U$ with valuation $-n$ are those in $p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$. Therefore:
$$p^{-n} + p^N\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq p^{-n} + p^n\mathbb{Z}_p,$$
which requires $p^N\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq p^n\mathbb{Z}_p$, i.e., $N \geq n$. But we chose $n > N$, a contradiction.

Since every compact open subgroup of $\mathbb{Q}_p$ is $p^N\mathbb{Z}_p$ for some $N \geq 0$, and no such $N$ works, $U$ cannot be expressed as a union of cosets of any compact open subgroup.

### Conclusion

By the equivalence, there is no neighborhood $V$ of the identity such that $gV \subset U$ for all $g \in U$. The answer is **no**: not every clopen subset of a t.d.l.c. group can be expressed as a union of cosets of a compact open subgroup.

